     I don't remember the scroll bug, but I don't think the
"additional info" here is accurate.  I think the problem
there was that the helm's enchantment was reduced while it
was worn, without the side-effect of having the hero's stats
be reduced too.  Then when the helm was removed for any
reason, the effect of its enchantment was reversed, so a
different value was subtracted from what had been added when
the helm was put on.  (Enchant armor doesn't suffer from that.)

     This new bug is entirely unrelated.  The analysis by
Ralf was correct; I just wish he'd posted it before I'd
already done the same work myself....  When putting on armor
that takes multiple turns to wear, it is marked as worn (and
affects AC) immediately, but any side-effects it has are
deferred until after the delay of N turns has finished.  The
'afternmv' function pointer is set to the routine which will
apply those side-effects.  But theft of armor which takes
more than one turn to take off also uses 'afternmv' to finish
taking that off, and setting it for the latter clobbered the
finish-putting-on step.  So the armor was worn but magical
side-effects of wearing it had not been applied, and when it
is later taken off, those side-effects got reversed as if
they had been.  For intrinsics and the like you won't have
them while the item is worn so might notice, but won't suffer
any problems when it is later taken off.  For non-zero stat
adjustment the reversal when it's taken off is obvious.

     I implemented a fix for this yesterday.  Successful
theft will interrupt and cancel the attempt to put on or
take off armor before the stealing code clobbers 'afternmv'.
And give a message that you stop putting on or taking off
the piece of armor (unless you happen to be taking off the
same thing that the nymph is stealing, in which case the
time to take it off for her will be reduced by the time
already spent trying to take if off for yourself).

     Armor removal by succubus/incubus ignores wear/unwear
delay and happens immediately, so doesn't clobber 'afternmv'.
And taking off an item which is in the process of being worn
will cancel that process first (provided that 'afternmv' is
intact), so this bug won't occur for those demons.  But I
made their attack do the same interrupt and cancel with
the "you stop" message anyway.



